Enable the AUXADC, so the sensor r191 switched on can actually read.

r191 registered cpu-thermal, gpu-thermal and soc-thermal, and then every read of
thermal_zone*/temp returned -ENODATA. The zones were there; the numbers never
were.

The MT8163 thermal controller has no ADC of its own. It drives the AUXADC across
the AHB bus, which means it has to be programmed with the physical addresses of
the AUXADC registers and of the sensor mux in APMIXEDSYS. All of that was
correct. Read back from the running device, every address matched the vendor's
mt_thermal.h exactly:

	TEMPPNPMUXADDR    0x1000c604   TS_CON1_P
	TEMPADCMUXADDR    0x1100100c   AUXADC_CON1_CLR
	TEMPADCENADDR     0x11001008   AUXADC_CON1_SET
	TEMPADCVALIDADDR  0x11001040   AUXADC_DAT11
	TEMPADCEN         0x00000800   BIT(11), channel 11

and the sequencer was demonstrably running. TS_CON1 read 0x1, which is the value
the hardware itself had written there from TEMPADCPNP1, and AUXADC_CON1 read
0x800, the channel enable it had written through TEMPADCENADDR. Both AHB write
paths worked. Both clocks were on: infra_auxadc at 26MHz and infra_therm at
136.5MHz, both held by 1100b000.thermal.

Only AUXADC_DAT11 stayed 0. Nothing was converting.

The reason is that mt8163.dtsi leaves auxadc@11001000 disabled. The converter is
powered by MT6577_AUXADC_PDN_EN - MISC 0x94, bit 14 - and the only code in the
tree that sets it is mt6577_auxadc's probe. With the node disabled that driver
never probed, no platform device was ever created, and the bit stayed clear.
Nothing in the thermal driver sets it; it assumes the AUXADC node is enabled,
which is what mt8173.dtsi does upstream.

Why a dead ADC surfaces as -ENODATA rather than as a wrong temperature is worth
recording, because it sent this investigation down two wrong paths first. The
driver masks the measurement to 12 bits, so a dead ADC reads raw = 0, and
raw_to_mcelsius_v1() with MT8163_CALIBRATION converts raw 0 to roughly +443C.
That is outside MT8173_TEMP_MIN..MT8173_TEMP_MAX, so mtk_thermal_temp_is_valid()
rejects it and substitutes THERMAL_TEMP_INVALID, which thermal_helpers.c turns
into -ENODATA. Stuck-low and stuck-high both land outside the window - a real
reading is raw 2501..3951 - so the error says "no data" no matter how the ADC
fails.

Two plausible-looking explanations were eliminated on the way, and are recorded
so they are not chased again. The apmixed sensor-buffer control that mt8365 sets
(TS_CON0 bits 29:28) does not apply: TS_CON0 reads 0x00000000, already the state
mt8365 programs. And the calibration is fine - the efuse cell is present and
non-zero, and in any case the driver starts from working defaults.

Verified by setting that one bit by hand on the running device before writing
this:

	AUXADC_DAT11  0x00000000 -> 0x00000dda
	TEMP_MSR0..2  raw 0      -> raw 3540..3555
	zones         -ENODATA   -> 33.7C / 33.6C / 33.6C

CONFIG_MEDIATEK_MT6577_AUXADC was already set, but as a module. It is built in
here instead. The thermal core is built in and registers zones long before
userspace could load anything, and this is the only thing standing between a
sealed enclosure driving a speaker amplifier and an unthrottled SoC; leaving it
as a module would leave a window at every boot where the trip points exist and
the sensor reads nothing.

